"<?xml version=\"1.0\" ?>\n<!DOCTYPE PubmedArticleSet PUBLIC \"-//NLM//DTD PubMedArticle, 1st January 2025//EN\" \"https://dtd.nlm.nih.gov/ncbi/pubmed/out/pubmed_250101.dtd\">\n<PubmedArticleSet>\n<PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status=\"MEDLINE\" Owner=\"NLM\" IndexingMethod=\"Curated\"><PMID Version=\"1\">34090384</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2021</Year><Month>06</Month><Day>10</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2021</Year><Month>06</Month><Day>10</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel=\"Electronic\"><Journal><ISSN IssnType=\"Electronic\">1471-2334</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium=\"Internet\"><Volume>21</Volume><Issue>1</Issue><PubDate><Year>2021</Year><Month>Jun</Month><Day>05</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>BMC infectious diseases</Title><ISOAbbreviation>BMC Infect Dis</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Klebsiella spp. cause severe and fatal disease in Mozambican children: antimicrobial resistance profile and molecular characterization.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>526</StartPage><MedlinePgn>526</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType=\"pii\" ValidYN=\"Y\">526</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType=\"doi\" ValidYN=\"Y\">10.1186/s12879-021-06245-x</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText Label=\"BACKGROUND\" NlmCategory=\"BACKGROUND\">Klebsiella spp. are important pathogens associated with bacteremia among admitted children and is among the leading cause of death in children &lt;&#x2009;5&#x2009;years in postmortem studies, supporting a larger role than previously considered in childhood mortality. Herein, we compared the antimicrobial susceptibility, mechanisms of resistance, and the virulence profile of Klebsiella spp. from admitted and postmortem children.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label=\"METHODS\" NlmCategory=\"METHODS\">Antimicrobial susceptibility and virulence factors of Klebsiella spp. recovered from blood samples collected upon admission to the hospital (n&#x2009;=&#xa0;88) and postmortem blood (n&#x2009;=&#xa0;23) from children &lt;&#x2009;5&#x2009;years were assessed by disk diffusion and multiplex PCR.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label=\"RESULTS\" NlmCategory=\"RESULTS\">Klebsiella isolates from postmortem blood were likely to be ceftriaxone resistant (69.6%, 16/23 vs. 48.9%, 43/88, p&#x2009;=&#x2009;0.045) or extended-spectrum &#x3b2;-lactamase (ESBL) producers (60.9%, 14/23 vs. 25%, 22/88, p&#x2009;=&#xa0;0.001) compared to those from admitted children. bla<sub>CTX-M-15</sub> was the most frequent ESBL gene: 65.3%, 9/14 in postmortem isolates and 22.7% (5/22) from admitted children. We found higher frequency of genes associated with hypermucoviscosity phenotype and invasin in postmortem isolates than those from admitted children: rmpA (30.4%; 7/23 vs. 9.1%, 8/88, p&#x2009;=&#xa0;0.011), wzi-K1 (34.7%; 8/23 vs. 8%; 7/88, p&#x2009;=&#xa0;0.002) and traT (60.8%; 14/23 vs. 10.2%; 9/88, p&#x2009;&lt;&#x2009;0.0001), respectively. Additionally, serine protease auto-transporters of Enterobacteriaceae were detected from 1.8% (pic) to 12.6% (pet) among all isolates. Klebsiella case fatality rate was 30.7% (23/75).</AbstractText><AbstractText Label=\"CONCLUSION\" NlmCategory=\"CONCLUSIONS\">Multidrug resistant Klebsiella spp. harboring genes associated with hypermucoviscosity phenotype has emerged in Mozambique causing invasive fatal disease in children; highlighting the urgent need for prompt diagnosis, appropriate treatment and effective preventive measures for infection control.</AbstractText></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN=\"Y\"><Author ValidYN=\"Y\"><LastName>Massinga</LastName><ForeName>Ars&#xe9;nia J</ForeName><Initials>AJ</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Centro de Investiga&#xe7;&#xe3;o em Sa&#xfa;de de Manhi&#xe7;a (CISM), Maputo, Mozambique.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN=\"Y\"><LastName>Garrine</LastName><ForeName>Marcelino</ForeName><Initials>M</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Centro de Investiga&#xe7;&#xe3;o em Sa&#xfa;de de Manhi&#xe7;a (CISM), Maputo, Mozambique.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Global Health and Tropical Medicine, Instituto de Higiene e Medicina Tropical, Universidade Nova de Lisboa (IHMT, UNL), Lisbon, Portugal.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN=\"Y\"><LastName>Messa</LastName><ForeName>Augusto</ForeName><Initials>A</Initials><Suffix>Jr</Suffix><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Centro de Investiga&#xe7;&#xe3;o em Sa&#xfa;de de Manhi&#xe7;a (CISM), Maputo, Mozambique.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN=\"Y\"><LastName>Nobela</LastName><ForeName>N&#xe9;lio A</ForeName><Initials>NA</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Centro de Investiga&#xe7;&#xe3;o em Sa&#xfa;de de Manhi&#xe7;a (CISM), Maputo, Mozambique.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN=\"Y\"><LastName>Boisen</LastName><ForeName>Nadia</ForeName><Initials>N</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Bacteria, Parasites and Fungi, Statens Serum Institut, Copenhagen, Denmark.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN=\"Y\"><LastName>Massora</LastName><ForeName>Sergio</ForeName><Initials>S</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Centro de Investiga&#xe7;&#xe3;o em Sa&#xfa;de de Manhi&#xe7;a (CISM), Maputo, Mozambique.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN=\"Y\"><LastName>Cossa</LastName><ForeName>An&#xe9;lsio</ForeName><Initials>A</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Centro de Investiga&#xe7;&#xe3;o em Sa&#xfa;de de Manhi&#xe7;a (CISM), Maputo, Mozambique.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN=\"Y\"><LastName>Varo</LastName><ForeName>Rosauro</ForeName><Initials>R</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>ISGlobal, Hospital Cl&#xed;nic-Universitat de Barcelona, Barcelona, Spain.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN=\"Y\"><LastName>Sitoe</LastName><ForeName>Ant&#xf3;nio</ForeName><Initials>A</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Centro de Investiga&#xe7;&#xe3;o em Sa&#xf